{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "B-IUUwtR-0P3cfOEr",
	"notes": "A Warboss: the biggest, meanest, canniest Ork running a Waaagh. Point math: Ork Physiology 251 + Growth Lens: Warboss 320 (which replaces the Nob lens and carries Injury Tolerance divisor 4 in total) + skills 96 = 667 points. Stats: ST 32, DX 12, IQ 10, HT 15, HP 40, Will 10, Per 10, Move 11, SM +1 — framework.md's Warboss anchor line exactly. This lands in the lower half of framework.md's Astartes tier (550-800): a solid, competent Warboss who genuinely out-muscles a Space Marine, built mostly from sheer physical scale, battlefield toughness and raw command presence (see Charisma and Wealth in the Growth Lens) rather than the breadth of skill and equipment a Chapter gives its Battle-Brothers. A legendary, named Warboss (a Ghazghkull Thraka-tier figure) should be built as a bespoke NPC well above this template, in framework.md's Heroes and Legends tier (1,000-3,000) — this is the \"typical, dangerous Warboss\" baseline, not the biggest name in the setting. Enable at most one Klan lens for flavor.\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "TShA_BBFEc8BY3gBK",
			"name": "Ork Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork Boy: the baseline greenskin warrior, grown fast, fed on violence, and built to get bigger and meaner the longer it survives. This trait is the shared baseline every growth lens (Nob, Warboss) and every Oddboy specialisation (Mek, Painboy, Weirdboy) builds on top of. Primary sources: Codex: Orks background across editions, Nate Crowley's Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! and Mike Brooks' Brutal Kunnin', calibrated against Dark Heresy/Rogue Trader/Deathwatch/Only War Ork material for relative scale.\n\nDesign: 138 (Attribute Modifiers) + 15 (Basic Move +3) + 10 (Thick Hide) + 10 (High Pain Threshold) + 6 (Hard to Kill 3) + 5 (Rapid Healing) + 6 (Thick Skull) + 15 (Resistant to Disease and Toxins) + 0 (Fungal Physiology) - 10 (Bloodlust) - 10 (Impulsiveness) - 5 (Overconfidence) + 10 (Ork Kunnin' 1) + 5 (WAAAGH! Gestalt) + 6 (Fearlessness 3) + 50 (Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction), divisor 2) = 251 points of physiology alone, before skills — see the Ork Boy template for a full character, and the growth lenses and Oddboy Talents below for Nobs, Warbosses, Meks, Painboyz and Weirdboyz built on top of it.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t_GJeJMUnRXYOTmHV",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Boy)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: ST 22, DX 11, IQ 8, HT 13, HP 26, SM 0. Increased ST +12 (B14, 10/level, 120) for ST 22 — \"stooped but heavy; brawnier than a man\": Basic Lift 96 lb, well past an unaugmented human but nowhere near an Astartes' ST 30. Increased DX +1 (B15, 20/level, 20) for DX 11 — \"far clumsier than a Marine,\" barely above a Guardsman's own DX 12 despite the far greater ST. Decreased IQ -2 (B16, 20/level, -40) for IQ 8, the framework's own figure for \"not smart, but not helpless either\" — offset in play by Ork Kunnin' below rather than by raw IQ. Increased HT +3 (B15, 10/level, 30) for HT 13. Extra Hit Points +4 (B16, 2/level, 8) for HP 26 (22 base from ST, +4): an Ork soaks up more damage than its ST alone would suggest, matching how much punishment a Boy shrugs off in the fiction. 120+20-40 +30+8 = 138 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 138,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 12
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tfsklbqFyyDwfAqHl",
					"name": "Increased Basic Move (Ork)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Ork legs are short, but they cover ground at a lumbering, relentless gallop: a mob at full charge outruns a marching human line and is on top of it before it can reload. Codex background and Ork fiction alike treat the onrushing Waaagh! as a stampede, not a march.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Move +3 (B17, 5/level, 15 points): Basic Speed 6.0 gives a Boy Move 6, so +3 brings the Ork baseline to Move 9, faster than a human's 6-7 without approaching an Eldar's.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 3
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tCvdJdvEs6IRyxbJo",
					"name": "Thick Hide",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 2, all locations. Stacks with worn armour.\n\nLore: An Ork's skin is thick, rubbery and mottled green, tough enough on its own to turn a knife-edge or a glancing shot that would open a human. Every depiction of an unarmoured Ork brawling, in Codex background and Ork fiction alike, treats a bit of greenskin hide as meaningfully tougher than bare human flesh, well before any scrap armour goes on top of it.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance 2 (B46, 5/level, all locations, 10 points): modest next to the Ossmodula's partial DR 3 or Custodes-tier toughness, but applies everywhere and is on top of whatever armour an Ork straps on.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tTh57ZR9LM0dVRa_T",
					"name": "High Pain Threshold",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks shrug off wounds, keep fighting with an arm hanging by a tendon, and treat a lost eye as a minor inconvenience worth a good story later — a running joke across every Ork-focussed novel and a staple of Codex background (\"'e's still comin'!\").\n\nDesign: Basic Set High Pain Threshold (B59), 10 points, unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "t11ZNyjD9WJL83GgN",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "+3 to HT rolls made solely to avoid death.\n\nLore: An Ork that should be dead — gutshot, missing limbs, skull dented in — routinely keeps swinging until the fight is over, and often survives afterwards besides. Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! plays this for both horror and comedy in the same scene more than once.\n\nDesign: Hard to Kill 3 (B58, 2/level, 6 points): +3 to death checks, below a Warboss's own extra levels below.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t3cbLdBnqiWro2u9P",
					"name": "Rapid Healing",
					"reference": "B79",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Wounds that would fester or kill close over in days rather than weeks — part of the same fungal biology that lets a beaten Ork mob be back at strength within a season. Codex background is explicit that Ork tissue heals unnaturally fast; Black Library plays it as \"give it a week and 'e'll be grand.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rapid Healing (B79, 5 points): doubles effective HT for natural recovery rolls.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "t6rXPLumcimFfriv1",
					"name": "Thick Skull",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 4 on the skull only, on top of Thick Hide (skull DR 6 in total). Orks have brains and take normal extra injury from skull and eye hits once something gets through. House rule, not a priced trait: an Ork who loses a limb, or per the fiction even the head, to a crippling wound is not necessarily dead from that alone. A Painboy who recovers the part and staples or wires it back on within a scene or two restores it to function once the patient heals, using Surgery.\n\nLore: Ork skulls are notoriously thick, and Orks survive head wounds that would kill anything else, up to having a head sewn back on by a Dok (Codex background and a long tradition of \"'e's had worse\" anecdotes). They do have brains: a bolt through the eye still kills an Ork.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance 4 (B46, 5/level, 20) with Partial (skull only): the GCS Basic Set data prices Partial at -10% per -1 of the location's hit penalty, and the skull is -7, so -70%: 20 x 0.3 = 6 points. Replaced an earlier Injury Tolerance (No Brain), at the user's direction, because it made headshots meaningless against a species that does have a brain.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m2cGmOjtMvDpfr6q_",
							"name": "Partial (Skull only)",
							"reference": "B47",
							"cost_adj": "-70%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 20,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"skull"
							],
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tjSqaN1F-K49t05cV",
					"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Common: Disease and Toxins), Immunity.\n\nLore: An Ork can eat rotten meat, drink promethium-cut moonshine and wade through toxic industrial runoff without noticeable harm — Codex background treats Ork constitutions as effectively immune to the diseases and poisons that trouble other races, part of the same fungal-symbiote biology that lets a Squig be dinner, transport or weapon depending on how it's grown.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Common: Disease and Toxins, Immunity tier — base 15, x1 for Immunity = 15 points, the same tier and math this project's Oolitic Kidney entry uses for a single unified hazard category, pitched below the Custodes' broader Very Common tier (30) since Orks are shown getting stinking drunk and mildly green-sick on purpose for fun, not as flatly unaffected as a Custodian.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tLTEcnrOpwpVlJCBX",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated). The Nob and Warboss growth lenses raise the divisor through \"Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) upgrade\" levels; add the upgrade levels to this trait's 2 for the total divisor.\n\nLore: Orks survive and fight on through wounds that would kill a man: Codex: Orks background is full of Boyz who keep charging with holes in them, and the fungal-hardy greenskin body simply does not register damage the way a human one does. A big Ork is tougher still (see the growth lenses).\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 2 = 50 points. Bought as levels rather than a flat cost so the growth lenses can raise the divisor one step at a time. It is what keeps a Boy standing after two lasgun hits and makes bolt-pistol rounds a real but not instantly fatal threat, on top of Thick Hide's DR and the extra HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tvsssw3Oc3976xl36",
					"name": "Fungal Physiology (Spore-Born)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks are not born but grown: an Ork reproduces by releasing spores that seed fresh fungal growth wherever conditions allow (a mechanism shared with Gretchin, Squigs and the rest of the Ork ecosystem), has no biological sex, and needs neither a mate nor, ordinarily, a trained physician who isn't another Ork with a saw and a good attitude. This is consistent, well-established Codex background and is played straight rather than for shock across Black Library's Ork fiction — an Ork simply is an ambulatory fungal colony shaped like a green thug.\n\nDesign: 0-point lore flag. Every mechanical payoff of this biology (thick hide, extreme pain tolerance, fast healing, survivability of wounds that would kill outright, immunity to disease and poison) is priced separately above; this entry exists purely to document why an Ork needs no gender, no mate, and no bedside manner from anyone.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tMxpXaF_sQAZxc1Zu",
					"name": "Bloodlust",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Fighting is not a means to an end for an Ork, it is the point of being alive: Orks pick fights they don't need to, press an attack past the point of sense, and are consistently written (Ghazghkull Thraka above all) as unable to simply walk away from a fight worth having.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bloodlust (B125), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tpDSz2LDW6pkZYTOm",
					"name": "Impulsiveness",
					"reference": "B139",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks act first and, on a good day, think about it afterwards — a running theme in how Ork plans in the fiction go from \"good idea\" to \"already happening\" with nothing in between.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Impulsiveness (B139), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tvHiHk-8rFVzGDW3J",
					"name": "Overconfidence",
					"reference": "B148",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork mob genuinely believes it can win any fight it's in, right up until (and sometimes past) the point it's losing badly — a big part of why Ork strategy in the fiction is so often \"charge and find out.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Overconfidence (B148), self-control 12, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -5,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tEV1eB-vakc-HNtUS",
					"name": "Ork Kunnin'",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "This project's Talent (B89) for practical battlefield cunning: adds\nits level to Tactics, Traps, Camouflage and Observation, the same way\nthe Psyker Talent (Imperium/Traits/Psykers and the Warp.adq) adds to\nits own listed skills, per that file's house extension of Talent's\nnormal rules. An Ork Boy is not clever in the abstract (IQ 8 is IQ\n8), but a mob that has survived a hundred scraps has a bone-deep,\npractical instinct for where the enemy is weak, where the cover is,\nand when a trap will work — instinct this Talent represents directly\nrather than through raw IQ.\n\nLore: Ork tactics look like chaos from outside and are, by any human military-doctrine standard, chaos — and yet Ork mobs consistently find the gap in a defence, spring an ambush, or route around a strongpoint with an efficiency that surprises Imperial commanders who underestimate them. Codex background and Ork fiction play this as real, if inarticulate, battlefield cunning rather than dumb luck.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket), the same rate this project's Psyker Talent uses for the same reason. Base entry costs 0 points at level 0; every template above Gretchin buys at least level 1 (10 points, included in the Ork Physiology package below).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tRaQ1aGOwXyXrTJVB",
					"name": "WAAAGH! Gestalt",
					"local_notes": "Roughly ten or more fellow Orks fighting nearby, in earshot of the\nsame battle-roar, count as a WAAAGH!. While inside one:\n- Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp rolls get +2 (the conditional\n  bonus above).\n- Ork-built weapons and vehicles the character carries or crews have\n  their Malf number raised by 1 (fewer jams; e.g. Malf 14 becomes 15,\n  the Zzap Gun's 12 becomes 13). A lone Ork, or one with fewer than\n  ten fellows in earshot, gets no bonus. This is the mechanical hook\n  for \"Orks believe it, so it works, and it works better the more of\n  them believe it together.\"\n- A Weirdboy treats his own Warp Empowerment as one level higher (cap\n  +10) for the purpose of which known powers he may safely cast.\n  The Weirdboy template buys Empowerment 4 (cap 50), so Da Jump (60)\n  is castable only inside a WAAAGH!.\n\nLore: A lone Ork is dangerous; a mob of Orks worked up into a full WAAAGH! is a weaponised belief field — braver, louder, and (per Codex background's long-running joke about Ork technology) more likely to have its crude guns and rickety vehicles actually function, because enough Orks are collectively certain that they will. Weirdboyz in particular are consistently shown drawing directly on this collective psychic roar to power their biggest, most dangerous tricks; a Weirdboy isolated from a WAAAGH! is a much smaller threat.\n\nDesign: A conditional Fright Check/Perils bonus eyeballed against Basic's Fearlessness (2 points per level, so a flat +2 is 4 points, B55), restricted to a common-in-play situation (a mob of ten or more), which alone would price at about 2-3 points. The 5 points also buy two mechanical riders that are the point of the trait: an Ork-tech Malf +1 inside a WAAAGH!, and the Weirdboy's Empowerment +1. The Empowerment rider is not separately priced beyond that since it only changes which already-bought powers a Weirdboy may safely reach for.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "conditional_modifier",
							"situation": "to resist Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp, while fighting as part of a large mob of fellow Orks (a WAAAGH!)",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tiCAuh0hOFNh2-dCL",
					"name": "Fearlessness",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork has no fear to speak of: a lone Boy walks at a line of Marines because it looks like a good fight. It is the Gretchin who run. Below the Warboss's own extra levels, but enough that a Boy with Will 8 no longer fails a Fright Check as readily as a Guardsman.\n\nDesign: Fearlessness 3 (B55, 2/level, 6 points), a flat +3 to Fright Checks and Will rolls to resist fear and intimidation, on top of WAAAGH! Gestalt's conditional bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TtyMfgW4qT8SiRD4t",
			"name": "Growth Lens: Warboss",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss is the biggest, meanest, canniest Ork in charge of a Waaagh — a Boy or Nob who kept fighting, kept winning, and kept growing until nothing short of a Space Marine or worse could reliably put him down. Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! is the definitive Black Library portrait; current Codex: Orks background agrees a Warboss out-muscles a Space Marine on raw size and power alone.\n\nDesign: 166 (Attribute Modifiers) + 15 (Combat Reflexes) + 8 (Fearlessness 4) + 10 (Regeneration) + 6 (further Hard to Kill 3) + 15 (Reputation) + 15 (Charisma 3) + 10 (Basic Speed) + 5 (Basic Move +1) + 20 (Wealth) + 50 (Injury Tolerance upgrade 2, divisor 4 total; the Warboss lens replaces the Nob lens, so it carries both steps) = 320 points, added to the Ork Physiology package's 251 for 571 points of physiology and command presence before skills. See the Ork Warboss template, which lands the full character just under the low end of framework.md's Astartes point tier (550-800) — appropriate for a solid, competent Warboss; a legendary name like Ghazghkull himself would run well past this, into the framework's Heroes and Legends tier, and should be built as a bespoke NPC on top of this template rather than read off it directly.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tyPdUJsSylVC9rjJa",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Warboss)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target over the Boy package: ST 32, DX 12, IQ 10, HT 15, HP 40, SM +1. Increased ST +10 (B14, 10/level, 100) for ST 32; per B18's Size and Strength Table a creature whose SM matches its actual bulk gets a cost break on ST, and SM +1 gives -10% on every level of ST bought, including the Boy package's +12: the Warboss's whole +22 costs 220 x 0.9 = 198, of which the Boy package already charged 120, so this lens adds 78 — the same discount this project's Custodian Attribute Modifiers takes for the same reason. Increased DX +1 (B15, 20/level, 20) for DX 12 — a Warboss hits harder and a little more precisely than a rank-and-file Boy, \"out-muscles a Space Marine\" more than out-fights one. Increased IQ +2 (B16, 20/level, 40) for IQ 10 — sharp enough to run a Waaagh's actual logistics of loot, grudges and turf, on top of Ork Kunnin's practical edge. Increased HT +2 (B15, 10/level, 20) for HT 15. Extra Hit Points +4 (B16, 2/level, 8) on top of the Boy package's own +4, for combined extra +8 over the new ST-32 base of 32, giving HP 40. SM +1 carries no cost beyond the ST discount already taken. 78+20+40+20+8 = 166 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 166,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 10
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": 1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tK02pB2H3e-s9a5o8",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) upgrade",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Raises the divisor of the Ork Physiology package's Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) by two (this lens replaces the Nob lens rather than stacking on it, so it carries both steps: divisor 2 becomes 4): total divisor 4. The simulator sums the levels of the base trait and this upgrade.\n\nLore: A Warboss is tougher than any Nob; wounds that would fell a smaller Ork barely slow the biggest of the breed (Codex: Orks background).\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): 2 further levels, 50 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tdLToAa_zPue9esEk",
					"name": "Combat Reflexes",
					"reference": "B43",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss has been in more fights than any ten Boyz put together and simply doesn't flinch.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Combat Reflexes (B43), 15 points, as the Astartes/Custodes packages.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tZtDwdjFvQCrEr8dW",
					"name": "Fearlessness",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss is what other Orks are afraid of, not the other way around.\n\nDesign: Fearlessness 4 (B55, 2/level, 8 points), on top of the Boy package's Fearlessness 3 (7 levels in all) and WAAAGH! Gestalt's conditional bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 4,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tQ8FnurJQs6OXHX5k",
					"name": "Regeneration (Slow)",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Recovers 1 HP per 12 hours even without rest or first aid, on top of Rapid Healing.\n\nLore: The oldest, biggest Warbosses just don't stop knitting back together, a step beyond even an ordinary Boy's already-fast healing.\n\nDesign: Regeneration (Slow) (B80, 10 points), stacking with the Boy package's Rapid Healing as a distinct passive-recovery benefit, per this project's Custodian Regeneration entry using the same two advantages together.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tBh7P3iOt9MBRycOn",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "A further +3 to HT rolls to avoid death, stacking with the Boy package's Hard to Kill 3 for +6 total.\n\nLore: A Warboss dying on his feet is a running feature of Ork hero-tales, not an exception.\n\nDesign: A further Hard to Kill 3 (B58, 2/level, 6 points), stacking with the base package as GURPS allows multiple purchases of the same leveled advantage.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tI9_q0VQrty91dasc",
					"name": "Reputation (The Biggest and Baddest)",
					"reference": "B26",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss's reputation travels ahead of him — other mobs fall in behind him without a fight, and Imperial commanders who've faced a named Warboss before know exactly how bad the day is about to get.\n\nDesign: Reputation (B26), priced in line with this project's other eyeballed Reputation entries (Custodes' Shield-Captain Reputation, 15 points for +4) rather than a full frequency/scope calculation, per Basic's own guidance that Reputation costs are meant to be judged against comparable published examples.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "reaction_bonus",
							"situation": "from other Orks who recognise a Warboss's markings and retinue, and Fright Check bonus/penalty context for enemies who know his name",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tJQ6Inh5Y3lWzRJtf",
					"name": "Charisma",
					"reference": "B41",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Ork society has no formal rank structure; a Warboss leads because every Ork in earshot wants to follow him, fears him, or both — Codex background is explicit that Warboss authority is pure force of personality (backed by a fist the size of a Rhino) rather than any chain of command.\n\nDesign: Charisma 3 (B41, 5/level, 15 points), used in place of a formal Rank trait since Ork command has no such structure.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tmIee6sFnOMgSXLTx",
					"name": "Increased Basic Move (Warboss)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss leads the charge from the front; nobody outruns the Ork who is chasing them personally.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Move +1 (B17, 5/level, 5 points), on top of the Ork Physiology package's +3: Basic Speed 7.25 gives Move 7, so +4 in total brings a Warboss to Move 11.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t07W9EJjWRAk9t41u",
					"name": "Increased Basic Speed",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Warboss's reflexes stay sharp long after his mob-mates' have dulled from age or drink.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Speed +0.50 (B17, 5 points per 0.25 level, 10 points).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_speed",
							"amount": 0.5
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "ttXAkW4MCRSJAKpgR",
					"name": "Wealth (Loot Hoard)",
					"reference": "B25",
					"local_notes": "Wealthy.\n\nLore: A successful Warboss accumulates a genuinely enormous pile of loot over a career — teef, scavenged tech, trophies — the Ork measure of status and the fuel for the next Waaagh's gear.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Wealth (Wealthy) (B25), 20 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 20
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TB8Nnq4Ehh9C6RhF6",
			"name": "Klan: Blood Axe",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Blood Axes study and copy human (and other) military doctrine — formations, camouflage, combined arms — and are consequently distrusted by every other Klan as not being proper Orks at all.\n\nDesign: Net 0 points, per-Klan flavour lens, matching the Guardsman template's regimental-lens pattern.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "teiwBI3i9fce8NDzF",
					"name": "Human Tactics",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Blood Axe has actually studied how humans fight, and borrows what works.\n\nDesign: Perk (B9), +1 to Tactics.",
					"tags": [
						"Perk",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 1,
					"features": [
